  {"brand":"Hyundai","model":"Ioniq 5","price":"₹44.95 – ₹46.05 Lakh","fuel":"Electric","type":"Electric SUV","slug":"/hyundai-cars/ioniq-5/","tag":"Tech Leader"}
]'::jsonb,
'Why Buy an Electric Car in India in 2026',
E'India''s electric vehicle revolution is no longer a future promise — it is a present reality reshaping how the country buys, runs and thinks about personal mobility. Electric car sales in India crossed 1 lakh units per month for the first time in early 2026, with Tata Motors alone accounting for over 65% of the passenger EV market. Understanding the Indian EV landscape requires looking past the headline range numbers and examining what ownership actually costs, how charging actually works, and which vehicle suits which buyer.\n\nThe Tata Nexon EV remains India''s most popular electric car for compelling reasons. Its accessible price (starting at ₹14.49 lakh, net of FAME II subsidy where applicable), a proven electric platform that shares components with the petrol Nexon, and Tata''s rapidly expanding public charging network (Tata Power has installed over 4,000 public chargers across 300+ cities) make it the default recommendation for first-time EV buyers. Real-world range in mixed city-highway driving is approximately 280–320 km on the larger battery variant.\n\nThe Tata Punch EV at ₹10.99 lakh (base variant) is a significant milestone: the most affordable electric SUV in India that doesn''t feel like a compromise. The Punch EV platform delivers genuine performance — 0–100 km/h in under 9 seconds — while offering the lowest running costs in the electric car segment. At urban electricity tariffs of ₹6–₹8 per kWh, a full charge costs ₹100–₹160 and covers 150–180 km, delivering a per-kilometre energy cost well under ₹1.\n\nThe Mahindra XUV400 competes directly with the Nexon EV but targets buyers who want more range. Its 79 kWh battery (Pro variant) delivers a claimed range of 456 km — and real-world highway range of approximately 320–360 km — making it genuinely usable for intercity journeys between cities like Delhi-Agra or Mumbai-Pune. Mahindra has been aggressive in expanding its fast-charging infrastructure at highways.\n\nThe MG ZS EV positions itself as the premium alternative in the sub-₹25 lakh electric SUV space. British-Chinese brand MG (owned by SAIC) has built a cult following in India for its feature-loaded positioning, and the ZS EV benefits from this — offering an 8-inch ADAS system, a 50.3 kWh battery, and a comprehensive warranty (8 years / 1.5 lakh km on battery) that reassures buyers about long-term ownership costs.\n\nFor buyers who want to enter the EV world at the lowest possible cost, the Tata Tiago EV at ₹8.49 lakh is the answer. As India''s most affordable EV, the Tiago EV is ideal for urban-only use — its 315 km claimed range (approximately 200–230 km real-world) comfortably covers weekly city commutes, and its compact dimensions make it easier to park and charge than larger EVs.\n\nTotal cost of ownership (TCO) is where EVs genuinely disrupt the conventional car-buying calculus. A typical EV running 15,000 km annually in India spends approximately ₹18,000–₹24,000 on electricity (home charging at ₹6–₹8/kWh). A comparable petrol car covering the same distance spends ₹1.05–₹1.20 lakh on fuel. The annual saving of ₹80,000–₹1 lakh pays back the price premium of an EV over 3–5 years. When you factor in zero road tax (in several states), reduced maintenance (no oil changes, fewer brake replacements due to regenerative braking), and government purchase incentives, EVs present a compelling long-term financial case even at current prices.\n\nThe honest answer to "should I buy an EV?" in 2026 depends on where you park. If you have a dedicated parking space — apartment, house, or office — home charging is transformative. A 7.2 kW AC wallbox charges a Tata Nexon EV from 20% to 80% in approximately 3.5 hours overnight. If you park on the street without access to a dedicated charging point, EV ownership in India remains genuinely challenging, as public charging queues during peak periods can add 30–45 minutes to any planned stop.\n\nLooking ahead, the next 24 months will bring dramatic new entrants: Maruti Suzuki''s eVX, the Hyundai Creta EV, and Ola Electric''s first four-wheeler. The structural shift towards electrification in the Indian car market is irreversible — the question is no longer whether EVs will dominate, but how quickly.',
'[
  {"question":"What is the cheapest electric car in India in 2026?","answer":"The Tata Tiago EV is India''s most affordable electric car, starting at ₹8.49 lakh (ex-showroom). It offers a 315 km claimed range and is best suited for urban commuting. The Tata Punch EV starts at ₹10.99 lakh and provides more range (up to 421 km claimed) with an SUV form factor."},
  {"question":"How much does it cost to charge an electric car in India?","answer":"Home charging costs approximately ₹100–₹180 for a full charge depending on the battery size and local electricity tariffs (typically ₹6–₹8 per kWh in urban areas). This covers 250–400 km of driving. Public DC fast charging costs ₹18–₹25 per kWh and charges an EV to 80% in 30–60 minutes."},
  {"question":"What is the real-world range of Indian EVs?","answer":"Real-world range is typically 20–25% lower than claimed ARAI range. The Tata Nexon EV delivers approximately 280–320 km in mixed conditions; the Mahindra XUV400 Pro delivers 320–360 km; the Tata Tiago EV delivers 200–230 km. AC usage reduces range by a further 10–15% in summer."},
  {"question":"Is charging infrastructure good enough in India for EVs?","answer":"Charging infrastructure has improved significantly in 2025–2026. Major highways (Golden Quadrilateral, NH44, NH48) have fast chargers every 50–100 km. Urban areas with Tata Power, ChargeZone and Statiq networks have reasonable coverage. However, Tier 2 and Tier 3 cities still have gaps, and apartment-based charging (bulk metering, landlord permissions) remains a practical challenge."},
  {"question":"Does buying an EV in India have tax or subsidy benefits?","answer":"Several states offer road tax exemptions (0% road tax vs. 1–10% for petrol/diesel) and registration fee waivers on EVs. The central government''s FAME II scheme provides purchase incentives on qualifying EVs. Some state governments (Tamil Nadu, Delhi, Gujarat) offer additional cash subsidies of ₹1–₹1.5 lakh. Check your state''s current EV policy before purchasing."}
]'::jsonb,
true, 2),

