  {"brand":"Citroen","model":"Basalt","price":"₹7.99 – ₹13.83 Lakh","fuel":"Petrol","type":"SUV Coupe","slug":"/citroen-cars/basalt/","tag":"Coupe SUV"}
]'::jsonb,
'India''s Most Exciting New Car Launches of 2026',
E'The Indian automotive calendar in 2026 has been one of the most action-packed in recent memory. Manufacturers have responded to rising consumer sophistication by launching products that are genuinely more advanced, more efficient, and more feature-loaded than their predecessors — not just reskinned versions of older platforms.\n\nTata Motors'' Curvv is the most significant new nameplate from an Indian manufacturer in 2026. As India''s first production SUV Coupe, the Curvv combines SUV practicality with coupe aesthetics in a way that''s been commercially successful globally but had never been attempted by an Indian brand below ₹25 lakh. The Curvv''s swept roofline doesn''t sacrifice rear headroom as much as the design suggests — there''s still adequate space for adults up to 5''11'', and the boot capacity is a healthy 500+ litres despite the sloping rear. Available in petrol and diesel, with an electric version expected in late 2026.\n\nMaruti Suzuki''s fourth-generation Swift is the company''s most important new launch of the year. The Swift gets a new 1.2L Z-series naturally aspirated engine replacing the K-series unit — a significant change that improves refinement and meets the latest RDE emission norms. The new Swift is also lighter (down 20 kg from the previous generation) with improved torsional rigidity, resulting in noticeably better driving dynamics. The cabin gets a new 9-inch SmartPlay Pro+ infotainment system, a colour head-up display, and a 360-degree camera — features previously available only in cars costing ₹4–₹6 lakh more.\n\nHyundai''s Creta 2024 represents the most comprehensive overhaul of a segment-leading model in recent years. The new Creta offers three distinct personality choices: a conventional 1.5L petrol, a strong hybrid system (sharing technology with the Grand Vitara), and a fully electric variant (Creta EV). The interior is the most notable improvement — Hyundai has switched to a panoramic dual-screen layout (dual 10.25-inch screens spanning the dashboard) and added genuine Level 2 ADAS with highway driving assist, a first for a car under ₹20 lakh.\n\nKia''s Syros is the most aggressive competitive entry of the year. Positioned as an upper-compact SUV between the Sonet and Seltos, the Syros offers a feature set that makes established rivals look under-equipped. A panoramic sunroof, a 30-watt wireless charger, ADAS Level 2, and a 12.3-inch infotainment system come standard on mid-trim variants, not just in the top-of-range. The Syros enters the most competitive price band in the Indian car market — ₹10–₹15 lakh — and immediately sets a new benchmark.\n\nThe Skoda Kylaq marks Skoda''s entry into the sub-₹10 lakh segment for the first time in a decade. Built on the MQB-A0-IN platform (shared with the Kushaq and Slavia), the Kylaq benefits from Skoda''s reputation for solid build quality and European design sensibility at a price point that was previously the domain of Korean and Indian brands. The 1.0L turbocharged TSI engine delivers 115 bhp — significantly more than most rivals in this segment — and the cabin quality stands noticeably above the segment average.\n\nFor buyers navigating 2026''s new launch landscape, the core questions are: how much improvement does the new version offer over the outgoing model (facelifts often offer surprisingly little), what is the waiting period for delivery, and how has the launch affected the pricing of the previous generation (which often becomes available at significant discounts). The Indian car market in 2026 rewards the patient, well-researched buyer — multiple rounds of launches have created extraordinary value at every price point.',
'[
  {"question":"Which is the best new car launched in India in 2026?","answer":"For the ₹7–₹10 lakh segment, the Skoda Kylaq and Mahindra XUV 3XO represent the best new launches. In the ₹10–₹15 lakh segment, the Kia Syros and new Hyundai Creta set new standards. For buyers who want something truly new, the Tata Curvv as India''s first domestic SUV Coupe is the most exciting launch of the year."},
  {"question":"What is the difference between a facelift and a new generation?","answer":"A facelift (mid-cycle refresh) typically involves updated styling, minor feature additions, and tweaks to the interior — the fundamental platform, engine, and architecture remain unchanged. A new generation uses an entirely new platform, substantially redesigned engine options, and a ground-up interior redesign. New generations typically arrive every 5–7 years; facelifts happen at the 2.5–3 year mark."},
  {"question":"Should I wait for a new model or buy the current one?","answer":"If a confirmed new launch is 3 months or fewer away, waiting is worthwhile — you get a newer product without a depreciation hit. Beyond 3 months, the opportunity cost (missing use of the car, price uncertainty, possible long waiting periods post-launch) generally outweighs the benefit of waiting. If the current model is deeply discounted ahead of a new launch, that can also be excellent value."},
  {"question":"Which new cars in India have ADAS (driver assistance) in 2026?","answer":"Level 2 ADAS is now available in the Hyundai Creta 2024, Kia Syros, Kia Seltos, MG Hector, Volkswagen Taigun, and Skoda Kushaq, among others. Level 2 ADAS typically includes adaptive cruise control, lane-keep assist, autonomous emergency braking, and driver monitoring systems."},
  {"question":"Are new generation cars worth the premium over older models?","answer":"New generation models almost always use more fuel-efficient engines, offer better safety technology, and include features that simply weren''t available before. However, first-year production often has teething issues that get resolved in subsequent batches. Buying the second or third production batch of a new model (6–12 months after launch) often hits the sweet spot of newness and reliability."}
]'::jsonb,
true, 3),

